Note = {2025 International Symposium on Technology and Society-ISTAS-Annual,
   Santa Clara University, Santa Clara, CA, SEP 10-12, 2025},
Organization = {Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc},
Abstract = {As artificial intelligence continues to evolve, so does the scale of the
   systems we build, often with little regard for the energy required to
   train, fine-tune, and deploy them. From massive language models to
   real-time applications, the environmental cost of AI has quietly grown
   into a critical issue. This paper provides a closer examination of Green
   AI-an emerging approach that aims to reduce AI's energy consumption and
   carbon footprint without compromising performance. This study outlines
   actionable paths toward sustainable AI development by examining recent
   work in model architecture design, training efficiency, and deployment
   practices. In particular, it highlights the growing role of metrics such
   as energy per FLOP and carbon efficiency and calls for greater
   transparency through standardized emissions reporting. The paper also
   argues that closer collaboration between academia and industry will be
   essential for translating research into real-world impact. Ultimately,
   the goal is not to scale less, but smarter, with sustainability at the
   core of defining progress in AI.},
