   Security-ICDMIS, University of Puthisastra, Phnom Penh, CAMBODIA, OCT
   07-08, 2025},
Organization = {Eminent College of Management and Technology; International Knowledge
   Research Foundation},
Abstract = {Edge and real-time computing are transforming the way we approach data
   processing. They make it possible to do it much closer to the pipeline,
   with much less lag and network congestion. This paper examines the
   primary challenges, innovations, and anticipated trends in edge
   computing, particularly in relation to artificial intelligence, 5G
   networks, serverless architectures, blockchain security, and sustainable
   computing. It looks at the pros and cons of inference speed and
   precision and contrasts edge AI and cloud-based model architectures. The
   authors evaluated performance improvements via federated learning
   scalability, reduced 5G latency, and the balance of workload between
   edge and cloud. The study also looks at blockchain as a way to protect
   edge transactions, private computing methods, and green AI developments
   that aim to make processing more energy-efficient. It also looks into
   how quantum computing could be used for real-time AI inference and how
   it might affect edge computing in the future. This paper examines
   innovative and critical dimensions of edge intelligence development
   through realistic simulations, utilizing both synthetic and empirical
   datasets. The results show how to improve edge computing in the future
   by making systems that are strong, safe, and able to make decisions in
   real time.},
